    "slug": "taylor-swift-patient-zero-hot-100-billboard-top-five",
    "title": "Taylor Swift's \"Patient Zero\" Holds the Hot 100 as Country and Hip-Hop Collide in the Top Five",
    "excerpt": "Some chart weeks feel routine. This is not one of them. On the Billboard Hot 100 dated October 17, Taylor Swift's \"Patient Zero\" stays at No. 1 for a second straight week, Ella Langley's \"Choosin' Texas\" is ...",
    "content": "<p class=\"lead-text\">Some chart weeks feel routine. This is not one of them. On the Billboard Hot 100 dated October 17, Taylor Swift's \"Patient Zero\" stays at No. 1 for a second straight week, Ella Langley's \"Choosin' Texas\" is still sitting at No. 2 after almost a year on the chart, and a Drake and Don Toliver reimagining of that very song crashes in at No. 5. If you want a single snapshot of mainstream music in the autumn of 2026, this is a pretty good one.</p>\n\n    <h2>A Fast Start and a Steep Drop</h2>\n    <p>\"Patient Zero\" is one of four new songs on The Life of a Showgirl: The Encore, the deluxe edition of Swift's 2025 album, which arrived on September 25. It opened at No. 1 on the chart dated October 6, becoming her 16th career Hot 100 leader and ending the record-setting 24-week reign of \"Choosin' Texas.\" It was also her third new chart-topper of 2026, following \"Opalite\" and \"I Knew It, I Knew You.\" Billboard noted that the other three Encore additions, \"Cleveland!,\" \"Babylon\" and \"Pink Clouding,\" landed in the top five alongside it, which meant Swift briefly held four of the five highest positions on the entire chart.</p>\n\n    <p>The second week tells a more nuanced story. \"Patient Zero\" held firm, but its siblings did not. \"Pink Clouding,\" which sat at No. 5 a week earlier, has tumbled to No. 38 according to the weekly tracker Top40Weekly. That is the familiar arc of a deluxe-edition release: a huge opening driven by devoted fans who stream every new track on day one, followed by a sharp narrowing as everyone else settles on the single they actually want to replay. The lead song clearly earned its place, and that is the part that matters for the long run.</p>\n\n    <p>The milestones keep stacking up regardless. With 16 No. 1 hits, Swift now trails only The Beatles, who have 20, and Mariah Carey, who has 19. She has also tied Drake for the most songs to debut at the top of the Hot 100, with ten apiece.</p>\n\n    <h2>The Rest of the Top Ten</h2>\n    <p>Behind Swift, the story is mostly one of endurance. Langley's \"Choosin' Texas\" is now in its 51st week on the Hot 100, a run that includes the longest stay at No. 1 the chart has ever recorded, overtaking Mariah Carey's seasonal favourite. Stella Lefty's \"Boston\" has climbed to No. 3, while Morgan Wallen's \"Been By Now\" sits at No. 4. Then comes the week's big new entry: Drake and Don Toliver's \"Solar Eclipse\" debuts at No. 5, a flip of the Langley hit that is sitting three places above it.</p>\n\n    <p>Tame Impala's \"Dracula\" holds No. 6 in its 54th week on the chart, a remarkable run for a song that stopped being new a long time ago. Ariana Grande's \"Hate That I Made You Love Me\" (No. 7) and Olivia Rodrigo's \"Stupid Song\" (No. 8) represent the summer's pop heavyweights, both still performing months after their debuts. ADÉLA's \"Nicole Kidman\" at No. 9 is the most interesting newcomer, giving her a first top ten. Karol G's \"Bby Wow,\" featuring Judeline and Rusowsky, closes the list at No. 10, and Drake's \"Quebec\" enters just outside it at No. 11.</p>\n\n    <h2>What the Chart Is Really Telling Us</h2>\n    <p>Three things stand out. First, longevity is back. Two songs in this top ten have now spent more than fifty weeks on the Hot 100, which would have been unthinkable for most hits a decade ago. In the streaming age, a song that connects with a wide audience can simply refuse to leave, and that changes how labels plan release calendars.</p>\n\n    <p>Second, genre borders are thinner than they have ever been. The biggest hip-hop story of the week is built on a country song, and a country singer is one of the main beneficiaries. Third, the deluxe edition has become a serious competitive tool. Swift's Encore arrived on September 25 and Drake's expanded HABIBTI (FOMO) followed on October 2, and between them they produced the two highest-ranking new entries of the month. Releasing new material under the banner of an existing album gives artists a built-in audience on day one while keeping the original project alive on the album charts.</p>\n\n    <h2>What to Watch Next</h2>\n    <p>The obvious question is whether \"Patient Zero\" can survive a third week with Langley one spot behind and Drake's momentum still building. Swift needs four more No. 1 songs to match The Beatles, which sounds distant until you remember she has collected three new ones in a single calendar year. For now, the Hot 100 belongs to a pop superstar, a country phenomenon that will not quit, and a rapper who borrowed her best song. Check back next Tuesday, because this story is not finished.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"blog-highlight-box\">\n      <h3>Experience High-Fidelity Streaming on GanaTube</h3>\n      <p>Stream your favorite artists with studio-master acoustic clarity and zero audio interruptions. Create real-time <strong>Listen Together</strong> rooms with friends, explore curated charts, and enjoy continuous playback without mandatory registrations on <a href=\"/\">GanaTube.in</a>.</p>\n    </div>",
